"Understanding Outer Optimizers in Local SGD: Learning Rates, Momentum, and Acceleration (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10439v1)\nciteturn29777view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view5\",\"lineno\":160}); Total lines: 1234\nL154: This is because increasing $H$ incurrs additional client drift that slows down the convergence of the algorithm in the presence of stochastic gradient noise. When the outer stepsize $\\gamma$ is allowed to vary, the convergence of the algorithm is less clear. cite58†Karimireddy et al. (2020) gives the following convergence rate in the absence of data heterogeneity,\nL155:  | $\\displaystyle\\mathbb{E}\\left[f\\left(\\frac{1}{R}\\sum_{r=0}^{R-1}x_{r}\\right)\\right]-f(x_{\\ast})\\leq\\mathcal{O}\\left(\\frac{L{\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{R}+\\frac{\\sigma\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}{\\sqrt{MR}}\\right),$  |\nL156: for specially chosen $\\eta$ and $\\gamma$ pairs. This rate matches that of Minibatch SGD, but does not recover the convergence rate of vanilla Local SGD given by cite86†Equation 2 . cite50†Jhunjhunwala et al. (2023) also give a guarantee for Generalized Local SGD with a specific outer learning rate that is always at least $1$ and that depends on the heterogeneity of the iterates across the different clients.\nL157: Since the analysis is conducted in the heterogeneous setting, the local stepsize required to scale with $1/H$. A guarantee that applies to any outer learning rate in the nonconvex, heterogeneous setting given by (cite51†Sun et al., 2024 ).\nL158: The limiting factor in existing analysis is that we are forced to choose the local stepsize $\\eta$ to scale as $\\frac{1}{LH}$, whereas to obtain cite86†Equation 2 we sometimes need to choose $\\eta$ to be much larger, on the order of $\\frac{1}{L}$. If we aim to accurately characterize the convergence of (cite85†GEN-LOC-SGD ), our analysis has to encompass both large and small local stepsizes $\\eta$.\nL159: \nL160: New analysis. We now present our main convergence theorem for (cite85†GEN-LOC-SGD ).\nL161: ###### Theorem 3.3.\nL162: \nL163: Suppose that cite87†Assumptions 3.1 and cite88†3.2 hold. Then the iterates generated by Generalized Local SGD run with local stepsize $\\eta>0$ and outer stepsize $\\gamma>0$ for $R$ communication rounds and with $H$ local steps per round satisfy,\nL164:  | $\\displaystyle\\!\\mathbb{E}\\!\\left[f\\!\\left(\\!\\frac{1}{RH}\\!\\sum_{r=0}^{R-1}\\!\\sum_{h=0}^{H-1}\\!y_{r,h}\\!\\right)\\!\\right]\\!-f(x_{\\ast})\\leq\\mathcal{O}\\left(\\!\\frac{{\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{\\eta\\gamma RH}+\\frac{\\eta\\sigma^{2}(1+(\\gamma-1)_{+})}{M}+L\\eta^{2}\\sigma^{2}H\\!\\right),$  |  | (3)\nL165: \nL166: where $(a)_{+}=\\max(a,0)$. and provided the stepsizes $\\eta$ and $\\gamma$ jointly satisfy $\\eta L(1+(\\gamma-1)_{+}H)\\leq\\frac{1}{4}$.\nL167: Implications of cite52†Theorem 3.3 . Before giving a proof sketch for cite52†Theorem 3.3 , we first discuss its implications. Observe that when $\\gamma\\leq 1$, we are allowed to choose $\\eta$ larger than $\\Omega(\\frac{1}{LH})$. This is crucial to obtain the rate of cite86†Equation 2 . Indeed, when $\\gamma=1$, the requirement on $\\eta$ reduces to $\\eta L\\leq\\frac{1}{4}$ and we can choose $\\eta$ following (cite45†Woodworth et al., 2020a ) as\nL168:  | $\\displaystyle\\eta=\\min\\left(\\frac{1}{4L},\\sqrt{\\frac{M{\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{\\sigma^{2}RH}},\\left[\\frac{{\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{L\\sigma^{2}H^{2}R}\\right]^{\\frac{1}{3}}\\right)$  |\nL169: Plugging this choice of $\\eta$ yields the convergence guarantee of cite86†Equation 2 . Alternatively, when $8\\eta L\\leq 1$, the stepsize requirement is met if we choose $\\eta\\gamma LH\\leq\\frac{1}{8}$ and we immediately get the Minibatch SGD guarantee. In particular, choose $\\eta\\!=\\!\\mathcal{O}\\!\\left(\\!\\frac{1}{RL}\\!\\right)$ and $\\gamma\\!=\\!\\mathcal{O}\\!\\left(\\!\\frac{\\gamma_{\\ast}}{\\eta LH}\\!\\right)$, the rate then becomes\nL170:  | $\\displaystyle f(y_{\\mathrm{out}})-f(x_{\\ast})\\leq\\frac{8L{\\left\\lVert x_{0}-x_{\\ast}\\right\\rVert}^{2}}{\\gamma_{\\ast}R}+\\frac{\\sigma^{2}H}{8R^{2}L}+\\frac{\\gamma_{\\ast}\\sigma^{2}}{4LMH},$  |\nL171: \nL172: where $y_{\\mathrm{out}}$ denotes the average over all iterations and clients as in cite89†Equation 3 . Then for $R$ large enough we can choose $\\gamma_{\\ast}=\\mathcal{O}\\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{LD^{2}\\sigma^{2}MH}{R\\sigma^{2}}}\\right)$ and this gives us the minibatch SGD rate\nL173:  | $\\displaystyle f(y_{\\mathrm{out}})-f(x_{\\ast})\\leq\\frac{LD^{2}}{R}+\\frac{\\sigma D}{\\sqrt{MRH}}.$  |\nL174: This confirms the intuition that at the extremes, manipulating the stepsizes $\\gamma$ and $\\eta$ allows us to interpolate between minibatch SGD and (vanilla) Local SGD, as observed by (cite75†Woodworth et al., 2020 ). In fact, cite52†Theorem 3.3 allows us to go a step further and get an explicit expression for the optimal inner and outer stepsizes depending on the problem parameters. This is given by the following proposition.\nL175: ###### Proposition 3.4.\nL176: \nL177: Let $h(\\eta,\\gamma)$ be defined as\nL178: \nL179:  | $\\displaystyle h(\\eta,\\gamma)=\\frac{D^{2}}{\\eta\\gamma RH}+L\\sigma^{2}H\\eta^{2}+\\frac{\\eta(1+(\\gamma-1)_{+})\\sigma^{2}}{M}.$  |  | (4)\nL180: \nL181: Consider the optimization problem:\nL182: \nL183:  | $\\displaystyle\\min_{\\eta>0,\\gamma>0}h(\\eta,\\gamma)\\qquad\\text{subject to}\\quad$  | $\\displaystyle\\eta L\\left(1+(\\gamma-1)_{+}H\\right)\\leq\\frac{1}{4}.$  |  | (5)\nL184: \nL185: The solution $(\\eta^{*},\\gamma^{*})$ is given by comparing the following two candidates.\nL186: \nL187:   1. 1.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDetecting Text Manipulation in Images using Vision Language Models (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10278v1)\nciteturn29777view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view0\",\"lineno\":103}); Total lines: 246\nL95: We evaluate GPT-4o which is a closed source VLM, Qwen-VL [cite66†Bai et al.(2025)Bai, Chen, Liu, Wang, Ge, Song, Dang, Wang, Wang, Tang, Zhong, Zhu, Yang, Li, Wan, Wang, Ding, Fu, Xu, Ye, Zhang, Xie, Cheng, Zhang, Yang, Xu, and Lin ] and Llama-Vision-3.2 [cite67†Grattafiori et al.(2024)Grattafiori, Dubey, Jauhri, Pandey, Kadian, Al-Dahle, Letman, Mathur, Schelten, Vaughan, et al. ] which are generic open source VLM, through zero-shot prompting task.\nL96: As shown in cite69†Fig. 1 , we prompt query image with a detailed prompt asking VLM to generate a final label. We further study the finetuned VLMs, FakeShield [cite46†Xu et al.(2024)Xu, Zhang, Li, Tang, Huang, and Zhang ] and SIDA [cite47†Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Hu, Li, He, Zhao, Peng, Wu, Huang, and Cheng ], on their generalization to text manipulation detection. We use their default prompts to get the prediction of the query image.\nL97: Additionally, we compare with a non-VLM baseline TruFor [cite38†Guillaro et al.(2023)Guillaro, Cozzolino, Sud, Dufour, and Verdoliva ], because it has a good generalization capability to unseen tampering methods.\nL98: ### 3.1 Datasets\nL99: \nL100: Most VLM-based manipulation detection methods lack a study on text specific manipulations. This task is challenging because usually text regions occupy smaller area in the image (cite70†Fig. 2 ). Therefore, we present their zero-shot evaluation on the following datasets:\nL101: #### Open-Set Text Forensics (OSTF) [cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ]\nL102: \nL103: dataset consists of images with in-the-wild text manipulation, which are sourced from $4$ different scene text datasets, and manipulation is done using $8$ different methods comprising text rendering, a convolution, and diffusion model-based editing. We use their test split with $2238$ images (1267 pristine, 971 manipulated) for our evaluation.\nL104: #### FantasyID [cite37†Korshunov et al.(2025)Korshunov, Mohammadi, Vidit, Ecabert, and Marcel ]\nL105: Text manipulation in sensitive documents poses severe security risks for KYC applications. This dataset has $362$ ID cards which mimic real-world IDs. IDs are printed on PVC cards and captured using $3$ camera devices. Unlike OSTF [cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ], the text-manipulated images by fine-tuning TextDiffuser-2 [cite71†Chen et al.(2024)Chen, Huang, Lv, Cui, Chen, and Wei ] on pristine cards. This creates difficult to detect manipulation as the artifacts (cite70†Fig. 2 ) are subtle.\nL106: For our evaluations, we use the $1572$ images ($786$ pristine, $786$ manipulated).\nL107: ### 3.2 Baselines\nL108: #### TruFor [cite38†Guillaro et al.(2023)Guillaro, Cozzolino, Sud, Dufour, and Verdoliva ]\nL109: uses a multi-branch Transformer encoder architecture to combine features from RGB images and Noiseprint++ images to predict an anomaly localization map, a confidence map, and a final score. Noiseprint++ [cite38†Guillaro et al.(2023)Guillaro, Cozzolino, Sud, Dufour, and Verdoliva ] is a fully convolutional network trained to extract subtle noise in pristine images caused by imperfections in camera hardware or in-camera processing steps.\nL110: When an image is manipulated, it creates a different noise pattern in the manipulated region, which can be treated as an anomaly. This is used to predict a final score for the image. Trufor can accept images of arbitary sizes but we consider two variants (a) TruFor-low longest side is resized to $512$ (b) TruFor-high, the longest side is resized to $768$ only for the images that cannot be processed in the original image dimension.\nL111: #### SIDA [cite47†Huang et al.(2024)Huang, Hu, Li, He, Zhao, Peng, Wu, Huang, and Cheng ]\nL112: is based on LISA [cite72†Lai et al.(2024)Lai, Tian, Chen, Li, Yuan, Liu, and Jia ] model, which the authors fine-tuned on a large dataset of manipulated images collected from social media. The model generates an explanation of manipulation along with its mask. We use the label provided by the model for evaluation. We use the default prompt with the query image. For input, the images are split into $4$ non-overlapping patches of size $336^{2}$ and then processed by vision encoder.\nL113: cite73†Image: Refer to caption Figure 3: VLM Prompt for FantasyID [cite37†Korshunov et al.(2025)Korshunov, Mohammadi, Vidit, Ecabert, and Marcel ] and OSTF [cite36†Qu et al.(2025)Qu, Zhong, Guo, and Jin ] We provide the necessary context of the problem along with intended output format.\nL114: #### FakeShield [cite46†Xu et al.(2024)Xu, Zhang, Li, Tang, Huang, and Zhang ]\nL115: proposes a fine-tuned LLaVA-v$1.5$-$13$B [cite68†Liu et al.(2023)Liu, Li, Wu, and Lee ] model for manipulation detection and localization, which is tuned on multimodal dataset of manipulated images to generate description of manipulated region along with its localization mask. The training data consists of edited images based on deepfakes, photoshop, and diffusion models. In our evaluations, we use the default prompt and consider an image manipulated when the model generates a mask.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSME-TEAM: Leveraging Trust and Ethics for Secure and Responsible Use of AI and LLMs in SMEs (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10594v1)\nciteturn29777view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view3\",\"lineno\":85}); Total lines: 239\nL79: Human–AI Integration  | Absence of oversight, interpretability, and trust mechanisms  | Limited human oversight fosters either distrust of AI outputs or blind reliance on flawed recommendations, heightening the risk of errors, security breaches, and poor decision-making.\nL80: ### 2.3 AI Risk Factors and Security Challenges\nL81: The rapid integration of AI and LLMs into business operations introduces a complex array of risk factors and cybersecurity challenges [cite40†18 ] that SMEs must address to ensure secure and responsible adoption. AI systems are highly susceptible to adversarial attacks, where carefully manipulated inputs distort outputs and lead to flawed or harmful decisions.\nL82: Threats such as data poisoning and model inversion attacks compromise the integrity and confidentiality of training datasets, while risks specific to LLMs, such as hallucinations and prompt injection [cite66†40 ], increase exposure to misinformation, manipulation, and unreliable outcomes.\nL83: Beyond these technical vulnerabilities, AI adoption magnifies broader challenges including algorithmic bias, opacity in decision-making, and over-reliance on automation, all of which can erode accountability and stakeholder trust. SMEs with their comparatively limited cybersecurity resources and governance mechanisms, are particularly vulnerable. Still many SMEs are not ready to adopt AI due to these risk factors and ethical issues [cite67†41 ].\nL84: This underscores the crucial importance of embedding secure-by-design principles, robust data governance, continuous monitoring, and structured human oversight across the AI lifecycle.\nL85: The identified gaps can be broadly classified into four domains: technical gaps, ethical gaps, operational gaps, and human–AI integration gaps, as summarised in Table cite68†1 . Addressing these gaps is essential to ensure that SMEs can adopt AI and LLMs in ways that are not only innovative and efficient but also secure, ethical, and trustworthy.\nL86: The multi-phased framework proposed in this paper is specifically designed to bridge these gaps, providing SMEs with actionable guidance across the pre-modeling, in-modeling, and post-modeling stages of the AI lifecycle.\nL87: ## 3 Conceptual Foundation for the Framework\nL88: ### 3.1 Trust and Ethical AI Principles\nL89: The adoption of AI in SMEs must be guided by a set of core trust and ethical principles that serve as both a moral foundation and an operational compass. Drawing on existing studies [cite69†42 , cite70†43 , cite71†44 , cite72†45 ], we outline the following key principles, which are interlinked and mutually reinforcing, to ensure that AI adoption promotes secure, responsible, and sustainable outcomes.\nL90: These are: Fairness and non-discrimination ensuring AI systems avoid bias and treat all groups equitably; Accountability and responsibility which require clear mechanisms of oversight, liability, and redress for AI-driven outcomes; Transparency and explainability which make decision pathways interpretable for both technical and non-technical stakeholders; Privacy and data protection safeguard personal information and ensure compliance with regulatory standards; Safety and security embedding resilience against adversarial attacks, misuse, and system vulnerabilities; Human-centredness and oversight emphasise the necessity of retaining meaningful human control, especially in high-stakes contexts; Inclusivity and accessibility ensure that AI serves diverse users and helps bridge digital divides; Trustworthiness and reliability highlight the importance of delivering consistent, dependable results that inspire confidence; Contestability guarantees that individuals, groups, or communities impacted by AI systems have timely avenues to challenge outcomes or decisions; and finally, Human, societal, and environmental wellbeing reinforces the principle that AI adoption should advance not only business efficiency but also the broader interests of society and the environment.\nL91: Together, these principles provide SMEs with actionable guidance to integrate AI and LLMs responsibly, balancing innovation with accountability and ensuring that digital transformation contributes to long-term resilience and sustainable growth.\nL92: ### 3.2 Framework Pillars: Mapping the Foundational Principles\nL93: The secure and responsible adoption of AI and LLMs in SMEs is grounded in four interdependent pillars - Data, Algorithms, Human, and Model Architecture, which collectively translate the principles of trust and ethics into actionable practices across the AI lifecycle in diverse SME contexts, as shown in Figure cite73†4 .\nL94: These pillars serve as the structural foundation for translating abstract values into actionable practices, enabling SMEs to effectively harness the potential of AI technologies, as discussed below.\nL95: cite74†Image: Refer to caption Figure 4: An illustration of SME-TEAM pillars with different application areas.\nL96: Data - is the foundational pillar of adopting AI technologies, as the reliability of AI outputs depends directly on the quality, authenticity, and provenance of inputs [cite75†46 , cite76†47 ].\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAbduct, Act, Predict:Scaffolding Causal Inference for Automated Failure Attribution in Multi-Agent Systems (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.10401v1)\nciteturn29777view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29772view4\",\"lineno\":134}); Total lines: 240\nL117:  | Algorithm-Generated (126 samples)  | Hand-Crafted (58 samples)\nL118: Method  | Agent Accuracy (%)  | Step Accuracy (%)  | Agent Accuracy (%)  | Step Accuracy (%)\nL119:  | Value  | Gain  | Value  | Gain  | Value  | Gain  | Value  | Gain\nL120: A2P (Ours)  | 65.40  | –  | 47.46  | –  | 58.62  | –  | 29.31  | –\nL121: Baselines  |  |  |  |  |  |  |  |\nL122: all_at_once  | 63.49  | -1.91  | 16.67  | -30.79  | 27.59  | -31.03  | 12.07  | -17.24\nL123: step_by_step  | 49.21  | -16.19  | 27.78  | -19.68  | 53.45  | -5.17  | 18.97  | -10.34\nL124: binary_search  | 46.83  | -18.57  | 28.57  | -18.89  | 44.83  | -13.79  | 13.79  | -15.52\nL125: On the more challenging Hand-Crafted dataset, our method achieves 29.31% step accuracy—a 2.43$\\times$ improvement over the all_at_once baseline’s 12.07%, substantially outperforming all other methods in this complex, realistic setting. The agent-level accuracy of 65.40% on Algorithm-Generated and 58.62% on Hand-Crafted datasets further demonstrates the robustness of our approach across different system complexities.\nL126: These results establish A2P Scaffolding as the first automated method to achieve nearly 50% step-level accuracy on algorithm-generated systems while maintaining superior performance on realistic, complex scenarios (cite21†Fourney et al., 2024 , cite20†Wu et al., 2023 ).\nL127: Research Question 1: How does structuring an LLM’s inference process with an explicit three-step causal framework (Abduction, Action, Prediction) and contextual step numbering affect its ability to perform fine-grained failure attribution in multi-agent conversations?\nL128: \nL129: Our systematic ablation studies provide compelling evidence for the necessity of each component in the A2P framework. Table cite56†2 quantifies the degradation in step-level accuracy when core components are removed.\nL130: Table 2: Impact of removing core causal components from A2P Scaffolding. Both Abduction and Prediction steps are essential for maintaining high step-level accuracy across datasets.\nL131: Configuration  | Algorithm-Generated  | Hand-Crafted\nL132: Step Acc. (%)  | Drop (pp)  | Step Acc. (%)  | Drop (pp)\nL133: Full A2P Model  | 47.46  | –  | 29.31  | –\nL134: A2P w/o Abduction  | 41.11  | -6.35  | 20.69  | -8.62\nL135: A2P w/o Prediction  | 40.32  | -7.14  | 17.24  | -12.07\nL136: The Abduction step, which enables the model to infer hidden causal factors behind agent actions, contributes 6.35 percentage points on Algorithm-Generated and 8.62 percentage points on Hand-Crafted datasets. This component transforms surface-level error detection into deep causal analysis by forcing the model to reason about latent variables such as knowledge gaps, incorrect assumptions, or misinterpretations that explain observed failures (cite48†Pearl et al., 2016 , cite49†Schölkopf et al., 2021 ).\nL137: The Prediction step demonstrates even greater importance, particularly for complex scenarios. Its removal causes degradation of 7.14 percentage points on Algorithm-Generated and a substantial 12.07 percentage points on Hand-Crafted step accuracy. This validates our theoretical framework that explicit counterfactual simulation—testing whether a corrective intervention would resolve the failure—is essential for distinguishing decisive errors from incidental mistakes.\nL138: The larger impact on Hand-Crafted systems suggests that counterfactual reasoning becomes increasingly critical as conversation complexity and length increase (cite57†Lewis, 1973 , cite58†Woodward, 2003 ).\nL139: Most remarkably, Table cite59†3 reveals the critical importance of contextual step numbering.\nL140: \nL141: Table 3: Critical impact of explicit step numbering on A2P Scaffolding performance. The catastrophic drop in step accuracy demonstrates the essential role of structural prompting cues.\nL142: \nL143: Configuration  | Agent Acc. (%)  | Step Acc. (%)  | Step Acc. Drop (pp)\nL144: A2P with Step Numbering  | 65.40  | 47.46  | –\nL145: A2P without Step Numbering  | 64.29  | 17.78  | -29.68\nL146: Note: Results averaged over 5 experimental runs on the Algorithm-Generated dataset (126 samples). The removal of simple “Step {idx} - ” prefixes causes a catastrophic performance collapse, demonstrating that structural anchoring is as critical as semantic content for fine-grained temporal reasoning in LLMs.\nL147: The removal of explicit step numbering—simply removing the “Step {idx} - ” prefixes—causes a catastrophic 29.68 percentage point collapse in step-level accuracy (from 47.46% to 17.78%) while leaving agent accuracy relatively unchanged. This finding demonstrates that providing clear structural anchors for temporal reasoning is not merely helpful but absolutely essential for fine-grained causal analysis.\nL148: The result aligns with recent work showing that LLMs’ reasoning capabilities are highly sensitive to input formatting and structural cues (cite60†Min et al., 2022 , cite61†Webson and Pavlick, 2021 ), suggesting that effective prompt engineering must consider both semantic content and syntactic organization.\nL149: Research Question 2: Can the A2P Scaffolding method achieve superior step-level accuracy compared to holistic, incremental, and hierarchical search-based attribution methods on both algorithmically-generated and complex hand-crafted agent systems?\nL150: Our comprehensive evaluation in Table cite55†1 demonstrates A2P Scaffolding’s systematic superiority across diverse system types and complexity levels. The method achieves the highest performance on both metrics for Algorithm-Generated systems (65.40% agent accuracy, 47.46% step accuracy), with step accuracy improvements of 2.85$\\times$ over all_at_once, 1.71$\\times$ over step_by_step, and 1.66$\\times$ over binary_search.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSmartCoder-R1: Towards Secure and Explainable Smart Contract Generation with Security-Aware Group Relative Policy Optimization (https://arxiv.org/html/2509.09942v1)\nciteturn29777view4 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"turn29771view1\",\"lineno\":225}); Total lines: 421\nL217: For S-GRPO, we build our implementation upon the Logic-RL (cite69†Xie et al., 2025 ) and VeRL (cite70†Sheng et al., 2025 ). We initialize from the L-COT SFT checkpoint and use a batch size of $8$ per device, a maximum prompt length of $24{,}576$, maximum response length of $2{,}048$, and a learning rate of $3\\times 10^{-7}$. KL loss with low variance is employed as a regularization term ($\\text{coef}=0.001$). Both the mini-batch and micro-batch sizes are set to $8$.\nL218: Gradient checkpointing and full parameter, gradient, and optimizer offloading are enabled to maximize GPU memory utilization. The number of parallel rollouts is set to $8$ (rollout.n=8). Rollout and reference log probability micro-batch sizes are set to $160$. Training is conducted on a single node with $8$ NVIDIA H800 GPUs (each with $80$GB memory), for $5$ epochs, saving checkpoints every $20$ steps. For Evaluation, we use greedy decoding (do_sample=false) to ensure stable and reproducible results.\nL219: This approach eliminates the randomness introduced by sampling-based decoding strategies, thereby allowing for fair comparison across different models and experimental runs. All evaluation experiments are conducted on the same hardware to ensure consistency.\nL220: ### 4.6. Experimental Results\nL221: \nL222: In this section we present experimental results and analysis to answer the research question.\nL223: \nL224: 1) RQ1: As shown in Table cite50†1 , SmartCoder-R1 achieves the highest overall performance across all key metrics when evaluated against a comprehensive range of baselines.\nL225: Specifically, SmartCoder-R1 achieves a ComPass (compilability) of 87.70%, improving over the best general/code LLM (GPT-4.1 at 84.13%) by 4.24% relatively and over the best domain-specific model (Storhaug et al. at 82.67%) by 6.08% relatively. On the critical VulRate metric (lower is better), SmartCoder-R1 attains a remarkably low vulnerability rate of 8.60%, which is 49.05% lower than CodeBC (16.88%), 44.80% lower than Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct (15.58%), and 72.09% lower than GPT-4.1 (30.82%).\nL226: For the SafeAval (compilable and secure) metric, SmartCoder-R1 achieves 80.16%, representing a 17.20% relative improvement over CodeBC (68.39%) and a 26.52% relative improvement over Storhaug et al. (63.36%). Furthermore, SmartCoder-R1 demonstrates notable improvements in FuncRate (functional correctness, 53.84%), slightly outperforming the strongest baselines (e.g., +1.76% relative over GPT-4.1 at 52.91%).\nL227: Especially, SmartCoder-R1 reaches a FullRate (functional correctness, security, and compilability simultaneously) of 50.53%, reflecting a 45.79% relative improvement over the next best result (DeepSeek-R1 at 34.66%). The seemingly low VulRate of some smaller models is an artifact of their frequent failure to produce compilable code (e.g., incomplete snippets or plain text), which are excluded from analysis.\nL228: Table 1. RQ1: Comparison of Secure and Functional Smart Contract Generation. Performance (%) of SmartCoder-R1 and state-of-the-art code generation baselines on secure smart contract generation benchmarks. $\\uparrow$ indicates higher is better, $\\downarrow$ indicates lower is better. Bold highlights the best results in each column.\nL229: Model  | ComPass $\\uparrow$  | VulRate $\\downarrow$  | SafeAval $\\uparrow$  | FuncRate $\\uparrow$  | FullRate $\\uparrow$\nL230: General / Code LLMs\nL231: CodeLlama-7b-Instruct  | 29.63  | 14.29  | 25.40  | 13.62  | 12.57\nL232: CodeLlama-13b-Instruct  | 54.63  | 23.24  | 41.93  | 25.13  | 21.83\nL233: CodeLlama-34b-Instruct  | 48.94  | 25.14  | 36.64  | 23.15  | 20.63\nL234: Llama-3.2-1B-Instruct  | 7.01  | 16.98  | 5.82  | 3.17  | 1.98\nL235: Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct  | 22.09  | 11.98  | 19.44  | 6.35  | 5.29\nL236: DeepSeek-Coder-6.7B-Instruct  | 15.08  | 21.05  | 11.90  | 4.89  | 4.37\nL237: DeepSeek-Coder-33B-Instruct  | 18.52  | 25.71  | 13.76  | 12.30  | 10.58\nL238: Qwen2.5-Coder-3B-Instruct  | 12.57  | 22.11  | 9.79  | 7.01  | 5.29\nL239: Qwen2.5-Coder-14B-Instruct  | 51.19  | 10.85  | 45.63  | 24.34  | 20.90\nL240: Qwen2.5-Coder-32B-Instruct  | 62.83  | 15.58  | 53.04  | 38.23  | 29.50\nL241: Qwen2.5-32B-Instruct  | 48.02  | 22.31  | 37.30  | 27.12  | 20.50\nL242: Qwen3-Coder-30B-A3B-Instruct  | 78.70  | 37.14  | 49.47  | 41.40  | 19.97\nL243: DeepSeek-V3  | 69.05  | 33.91  | 45.63  | 48.28  | 30.16\nL244: GPT-4.1  | 84.13  | 30.82  | 58.20  | 52.91  | 30.29\nL245: Domain-Specific Smart Contract LLMs\nL246: Storhaug et al. (cite43†Storhaug et al., 2023 )  | 82.67  | 23.36  | 63.36  | 34.79  | 23.54\nL247: Reasoning LLMs\nL248: DeepSeek-R1  | 84.39  | 32.29  | 57.14  | 50.66  | 34.66\nL249: QwQ-32B  | 67.46  | 44.12  | 37.70  | 40.48  | 23.94\nL250: SmartCoder-R1 (Ours)  | 87.70  | 8.60  | 80.16  | 53.84  | 50.53\nL251: 2) RQ2: Our ablation study in Table cite71†2 systematically dissects the contribution of each core component: Continual Pre-training (CPT), Long Chain-of-Thought Supervised Fine-Tuning (L-COT SFT), and Security-Aware Group Relative Policy Optimization (S-GRPO) to the overall performance.\nL252: Table 2. Ablation Study: Impact of Different Training Components. Performance (%) of SmartCoder-R1 variants with different training configurations. w/o: without.\nL253: Model Variant  | ComPass $\\uparrow$  | VulRate $\\downarrow$  | SafeAval $\\uparrow$  | FuncRate $\\uparrow$  | FullRate $\\uparrow$\nL254: w/o CPT & S-GRPO  | 80.82  | 19.80  | 64.81  | 31.22  | 20.37\nL255: w/o CPT & L-COT SFT  | 24.60  | 9.68  | 22.22  | 15.08  | 14.55\nL256: w/o CPT  | 85.05  | 14.15  | 73.02  | 37.17  | 31.75\n"
